WEBVTT

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:03.550
Pairing buddy, or Pair for short,

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:07.460
is a pair programmer that sits beside you in Neovim.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:12.310
It watches while you work, and speaks only when something matters.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:15.700
Its words appear where you are already looking.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.760
And it changes your code only when you hand it the wheel.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:23.210
This tutorial shows each of these, one at a time.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:28.100
First, the screen you will see in every scene.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.350
Watch the four labelled areas.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.450
On the left is Neovim, with your code.

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:39.140
Under it is your terminal, where you run commands.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.600
On the right is Pair’s chat.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.670
It keeps a record of what Pair says, and you can type to it.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:50.280
And along the bottom, the statusline shows what Pair is doing right now.

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:56.270
Your code, your terminal, the chat, and the statusline.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:58.200
Pair uses all four.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:05.370
Pair is a Neovim plugin and a small

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:08.690
program that runs in the background, the daemon.

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:11.200
Watch the last lines of each command.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:16.700
One npm command installs it, straight from GitHub.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:29.570
Then pair setup writes a config file from the example, offers a login,

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:33.820
and starts the daemon as a service, so it runs from now on.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:36.980
Last, it says what to add to Neovim:

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:41.430
the plugin, for lazy dot nvim, or any plugin manager.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.400
Two commands:

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.430
Pair is installed, and its daemon runs as a service.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:51.800
Pair needs a model:

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:55.900
a server on your machine, or a cloud provider with a key.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.430
Watch the ticks pair doctor prints.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.460
The config names one model for both jobs.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:06.670
This tutorial uses DeepSeek, in the cloud.

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:13.680
Pair auth stores the key, in a file only you can read.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.210
It never prints it.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:19.940
Then pair doctor checks every part:

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:23.440
the service, the daemon, the plugin in Neovim,

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.990
the sandbox for commands, and the model itself.

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:32.290
It asks the model a question, times the answer, and checks that it thinks.

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:35.840
A cross, instead of a tick, would name the fix.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:41.130
A config, a key, and pair doctor:

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:43.620
every part checked before you start.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.030
Two models share the work.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:52.690
The Watcher is small and fast.

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:55.730
It scores every moment from zero to ten:

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:57.460
is this worth a word?

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:00.160
Only high scores reach the Partner.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:04.720
It thinks first, reads your code, and writes what Pair says.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.210
One model can play both parts.

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:12.860
The Partner works in one of three roles, and the statusline always says which.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:14.860
Navigator is the default.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.710
It watches, and points at code.

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:22.880
Scout runs commands for you, in a pane of its own, inside a sandbox.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:27.370
And Driver types one change into your buffer, then gives the wheel back.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:32.720
You want to know why a test fails, without digging yourself.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.810
Watch the chat, and the pane that opens at the bottom.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:44.320
In the chat, slash scout switches Pair to the Scout role.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:48.480
Then you ask your question, in plain words.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:51.380
Pair’s own pane opens at the bottom.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:54.940
Scout runs the test there, and searches the code.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:58.860
Every command is typed where you can see it, in a sandbox:

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:02.970
only the project is writable, and there is no network.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.010
The answer comes back in the chat:

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:10.740
the greater-or-equal on line thirty-five drops the retry that fits exactly.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:15.410
You asked once; Scout ran the test in

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:17.860
plain sight and answered in the chat.

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:26.010
A test fails, and you start fixing the wrong file.

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:29.100
Watch the margin, under the line you change.

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:36.010
In your terminal, the delivery test fails:

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:38.700
it wants five attempts, and gets four.

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:44.120
You guess the backoff is wrong, and change the shift on line twenty.

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:47.810
Pair saw the red test,

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:51.170
and an edit in a file the failure doesn’t name.

00:04:51.170 --> 00:04:55.160
The Watcher scores that a wrong turn, worth a word.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:05:00.320
So the Partner reads the code, and speaks once, under the line you changed.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:04.750
The cause is the greater-or-equal in worker dot go.

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:07.170
Alt J goes where it points.

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:09.720
Your cursor never moves by itself.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:18.600
Pair spoke once, where you were looking, and pointed at the real cause.

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:26.690
You fix one line, and a test somewhere else breaks.

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:29.840
Watch the statusline, then the ghost text.

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:35.670
Each time you save, Pair runs your tests,

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:37.790
in its own pane at the bottom.

00:05:37.790 --> 00:05:40.340
The delivery test is red.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:47.230
You fix the check on line thirty-five, from greater-or-equal to greater, and save again.

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:52.930
Delivery passes now, but a queue test that passed before now fails.

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:57.100
That is a regression, and the Watcher scores it high.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.760
Pair says why, under your line:

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.060
the queue’s lease runs out just as the last attempt starts.

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:07.460
Alt J goes to its fix, in queue dot go.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:11.320
The ghost text shows the old line struck through,

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:13.180
and the new one under it.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.310
Nothing has changed yet.

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:17.790
Alt Y takes it into your buffer.

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:21.320
You save, the tests run again, and they pass.

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:38.950
Pair ran the tests you might skip, caught a regression,

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:41.340
and showed its fix before you took it.

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:49.090
You are writing a design note, not code yet.

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:50.880
Watch the margin of the note.

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:56.740
You finish a sentence about dead letters, and stop to think.

00:06:58.123 --> 00:07:03.453
When you stop, Pair reads the whole note, and the code it names.

00:07:03.453 --> 00:07:07.253
Its notes land in the margin, each with a sign.

00:07:07.253 --> 00:07:10.763
A question mark is a question the note leaves open.

00:07:10.763 --> 00:07:15.443
The not-equal sign marks two lines that can’t both be true:

00:07:15.443 --> 00:07:18.233
five attempts here, six there.

00:07:18.233 --> 00:07:21.653
The arrow says what the code really does.

00:07:21.653 --> 00:07:25.443
Here, the note is wrong about worker dot go.

00:07:25.443 --> 00:07:27.363
Nothing in the note changed.

00:07:27.363 --> 00:07:29.453
The marks are only in the margin.

00:07:32.413 --> 00:07:34.053
When you are ready to build,

00:07:34.053 --> 00:07:37.613
Pair tasks lists the work the note asks for.

00:07:37.613 --> 00:07:40.183
Nothing is added until you say so.

00:07:42.933 --> 00:07:45.293
Pair read the note like a colleague:

00:07:45.293 --> 00:07:49.423
questions, contradictions, facts, in the margin.

00:07:50.933 --> 00:07:54.033
An empty folder, and an idea.

00:07:54.033 --> 00:07:55.333
No code yet.

00:07:55.333 --> 00:07:57.823
Watch the note, under your last line.

00:08:04.113 --> 00:08:06.873
Pair new, and one sentence:

00:08:06.873 --> 00:08:08.263
what you want to try.

00:08:13.113 --> 00:08:18.263
Pair starts a note with your idea, and asks one question under it.

00:08:18.263 --> 00:08:21.373
You answer on the next line, in a few words.

00:08:23.223 --> 00:08:27.183
Each answer brings the next question, one at a time.

00:08:27.183 --> 00:08:29.533
Here, how the stall happens.

00:08:35.222 --> 00:08:37.423
And whether to cap the steps.

00:08:37.423 --> 00:08:39.513
Three short answers are enough.

00:08:47.676 --> 00:08:52.656
Then Pair suggests a first slice, dimmed, under your answers:

00:08:52.656 --> 00:08:56.096
two files, what they print, what to poke at,

00:08:56.096 --> 00:08:58.466
and what to leave out for now.

00:08:58.466 --> 00:09:01.246
Alt Y takes it into the note.

00:09:01.246 --> 00:09:04.096
Nothing is written to disk until you save.

00:09:05.946 --> 00:09:11.556
Three questions, three short answers, and a first slice small enough to start.

00:09:15.686 --> 00:09:20.306
You want the edge cases of a table test written, and you’d rather watch.

00:09:20.306 --> 00:09:22.876
Watch your code, and the statusline.

00:09:24.466 --> 00:09:27.976
You hand over with Pair drive, and say what you want.

00:09:28.946 --> 00:09:30.946
Pair is the Driver now.

00:09:30.946 --> 00:09:33.426
The statusline says it has the wheel.

00:09:33.426 --> 00:09:36.556
It reads the test and the code first.

00:09:36.556 --> 00:09:41.846
Then it types, into your buffer, where you can see every line arrive.

00:09:41.846 --> 00:09:46.626
It runs the tests on the unsaved buffer, in its own pane at the bottom,

00:09:46.626 --> 00:09:49.116
and hands the wheel back with a short note.

00:09:51.886 --> 00:09:54.046
Nothing is saved yet.

00:09:54.046 --> 00:09:57.086
U takes the whole change away in one step,

00:09:57.086 --> 00:09:59.456
and control R brings it back.

00:10:04.946 --> 00:10:10.256
Pair typed one change in plain sight, tested it, and gave the wheel back.

00:10:11.806 --> 00:10:14.886
Pair is typing, and you change your mind.

00:10:14.886 --> 00:10:17.516
Watch the lines it types, and the chat.

00:10:19.646 --> 00:10:21.696
Same hand-over as before.

00:10:28.886 --> 00:10:31.466
Pair starts typing the cases.

00:10:31.466 --> 00:10:33.226
You press any key.

00:10:33.226 --> 00:10:38.596
The typing stops, and what it typed is gone, as if it never started.

00:10:38.596 --> 00:10:42.826
The chat says you have the wheel back, and Pair is the Navigator again.

00:10:44.776 --> 00:10:49.486
Any key takes the wheel back, and nothing Pair typed stays behind.

00:10:53.676 --> 00:10:56.776
Your hands are on the code, and a question comes up.

00:10:56.776 --> 00:10:59.486
Watch the line under your cursor.

00:11:01.576 --> 00:11:04.876
Hold Alt T, and ask out loud.

00:11:04.876 --> 00:11:07.826
The meter shows Pair hears you.

00:11:07.826 --> 00:11:12.546
Let go, and whisper dot c p p writes down what it heard,

00:11:12.546 --> 00:11:14.246
on your own machine.

00:11:14.246 --> 00:11:17.756
That is your question, under your line.

00:11:17.756 --> 00:11:22.736
Then Pair answers in the same place, and points at line thirty-five.

00:11:22.736 --> 00:11:24.606
Alt J would go there.

00:11:28.616 --> 00:11:32.596
You asked out loud, and the answer came where you were looking.

00:11:32.596 --> 00:11:35.086
Voice is off until you set it up.

00:11:39.356 --> 00:11:45.026
Pair works with or without herdr, the terminal workspace for coding agents.

00:11:45.026 --> 00:11:48.446
Without it, one Neovim holds everything.

00:11:48.446 --> 00:11:50.266
Pair chat opens the chat,

00:11:50.266 --> 00:11:53.576
and Pair’s own pane is a terminal at the bottom.

00:11:53.576 --> 00:11:56.826
Every scene you just saw ran this way.

00:11:56.826 --> 00:12:01.426
With herdr, the chat and Pair’s pane are panes of their own.

00:12:01.426 --> 00:12:07.476
Pair also sees your other agents on herdr’s board, and terminals outside Neovim.

00:12:07.476 --> 00:12:11.326
Either way, it is the same Pair, with the same keys.

00:12:12.816 --> 00:12:14.976
Here are the keys to remember.

00:12:14.976 --> 00:12:21.236
Alt J goes where Pair points, and Alt Y takes its suggested change.

00:12:21.236 --> 00:12:25.416
Alt question mark explains the idea behind its words.

00:12:25.416 --> 00:12:29.656
Pair drive hands over one change, U undoes it,

00:12:29.656 --> 00:12:32.256
and any key takes the wheel back.

00:12:32.256 --> 00:12:35.056
Hold Alt T to ask out loud,

00:12:35.056 --> 00:12:38.746
and run pair doctor whenever something seems off.

00:12:38.746 --> 00:12:40.806
Pairing buddy is on GitHub.

00:12:40.806 --> 00:12:41.836
Happy pairing.
